### What is the problem with hardcoded access keys?

Static access keys are long-lived credentials that, if leaked through source control, logs, or a compromised instance, give an attacker unlimited access for as long as the keys exist. At National Bank of India, embedding AWS access keys in application code is a critical security violation because a single leaked key pair can reach every service the key is authorized for.

```java
// WRONG: hardcoded keys -- if this file leaks, the attacker has full access
package com.bank.service;

import software.amazon.awssdk.auth.credentials.AwsBasicCredentials;
import software.amazon.awssdk.auth.credentials.StaticCredentialsProvider;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;

public class InsecureClient {
    // Never do this in production
    private final S3Client s3 = S3Client.builder()
        .credentialsProvider(StaticCredentialsProvider.create(
            AwsBasicCredentials.create("AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE", "wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG")
        )).build();
}
```

### How does an instance profile work for EC2?

You create an IAM role with the permissions the application needs, wrap it in an instance profile, and attach it to the EC2 instance. The application running on the instance reads temporary credentials from the Instance Metadata Service, and the AWS SDK does this transparently via the default credentials provider chain -- you write no credential code at all.

```java
// CORRECT: no keys in code. The SDK picks up role credentials automatically.
package com.bank.service;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;

public class SecureClient {
    // Default provider chain: instance profile -> task role -> env vars -> ...
    private final S3Client s3 = S3Client.create();

    public void uploadStatement(String accountId) {
        // Uses temporary, rotating credentials from the attached role
        System.out.println("Uploading with role credentials for: " + accountId);
    }
}
```

### How does a task role work for ECS?

For ECS, you attach a task role to the task definition, and each task receives its own temporary credentials scoped to exactly what that service needs. There is also a separate task execution role that ECS itself uses to pull the container image from ECR and write logs to CloudWatch. It is important not to confuse the two: the task role is for your application's calls, the execution role is for the ECS agent's housekeeping.

```java
// ECS task definition with both roles (conceptual)
package com.bank.infrastructure;

public class EcsTaskConfig {
    // Task role: what the application can do (S3, DynamoDB, SQS)
    private final String taskRoleArn = "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/transfer-task-role";
    // Execution role: what ECS agent can do (ECR pull, CloudWatch logs)
    private final String executionRoleArn = "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/ecs-exec-role";

    public void printRoleExplanation() {
        System.out.println("Task role: authorizes YOUR application's AWS calls");
        System.out.println("Execution role: authorizes the ECS AGENT's housekeeping");
    }
}
```

### How does an execution role work for Lambda?

For Lambda, you attach an execution role, and the function's runtime environment provides temporary credentials automatically. The role grants both the permissions the function logic needs (such as reading from S3 or writing to DynamoDB) and the basic permissions to write logs to CloudWatch Logs.

```java
// Lambda handler using role-based credentials
package com.bank.lambda;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.dynamodb.DynamoDbClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.dynamodb.model.*;
import java.util.Map;

public class FeeCalculatorHandler {
    // SDK picks up Lambda execution role credentials automatically
    private final DynamoDbClient dynamo = DynamoDbClient.create();

    public String handleRequest(Map<String, Object> event, Object context) {
        dynamo.putItem(PutItemRequest.builder()
            .tableName("nbi-fee-records")
            .item(Map.of("txnId", AttributeValue.fromS(event.get("txnId").toString())))
            .build());
        return "Fee recorded";
    }
}
```

### How does Secrets Manager work?

AWS Secrets Manager encrypts secrets with KMS, controls access with IAM, and supports automatic rotation on a schedule using a rotation Lambda. The rotation Lambda changes the database password and updates both the secret and the database without downtime. Your application fetches the secret at runtime using the role's permissions.

```java
// Fetching a rotated secret at runtime
package com.bank.service;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.secretsmanager.SecretsManagerClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.secretsmanager.model.GetSecretValueRequest;

public class SecretFetcher {
    private final SecretsManagerClient sm = SecretsManagerClient.create();

    public String getDatabasePassword() {
        String secretJson = sm.getSecretValue(
            GetSecretValueRequest.builder()
                .secretId("prod/nbi-ledger/credentials")
                .build()
        ).secretString();
        // Parse JSON to extract username and password
        System.out.println("Fetched rotated credentials from Secrets Manager");
        return secretJson;
    }
}
```

### How does SSM Parameter Store compare to Secrets Manager?

SSM Parameter Store offers SecureString parameters, also KMS-encrypted and IAM-controlled, which is cheaper and suitable for configuration values and simpler secrets. It does not have built-in automatic rotation like Secrets Manager. Use Parameter Store for configuration like feature flags and non-rotating secrets, and Secrets Manager for credentials that must rotate automatically.

| Feature | Secrets Manager | Parameter Store SecureString |
|---|---|---|
| Encryption | KMS | KMS |
| Automatic rotation | Built-in (Lambda) | Manual (no built-in) |
| Cost | $0.40/secret/month + API calls | Free tier + lower API cost |
| Best for | Database passwords, API keys | Config values, feature flags |

```java
// Fetching a config value from Parameter Store
package com.bank.service;

import software.amazon.awssdk.services.ssm.SsmClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.ssm.model.GetParameterRequest;

public class ConfigFetcher {
    private final SsmClient ssm = SsmClient.create();

    public String getConfig(String paramName) {
        return ssm.getParameter(
            GetParameterRequest.builder()
                .name(paramName)
                .withDecryption(true)
                .build()
        ).parameter().value();
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is baking secrets into container images, environment variables committed to source, or AMIs. These are precisely where leaks happen. The second mistake is giving one overprivileged role to all services instead of a distinct, least-privilege role per service. The third mistake is never refreshing a cached secret, so the application breaks silently when Secrets Manager rotates the password.

```java
// Wrong: secret baked into environment variable in source control
// docker-compose.yml:
//   environment:
//     DB_PASSWORD: "MyS3cretPa$$word"   <-- committed to git!

// Correct: fetch at runtime, cache briefly, refresh on failure
package com.bank.service;

public class SecretCacheExample {
    private String cachedPassword;
    private long cacheExpiry;

    public String getPassword() {
        if (cachedPassword == null || System.currentTimeMillis() > cacheExpiry) {
            cachedPassword = new SecretFetcher().getDatabasePassword();
            cacheExpiry = System.currentTimeMillis() + 300_000; // 5 min cache
        }
        return cachedPassword;
    }

    public void onAuthFailure() {
        // Force refresh on auth failure (password may have rotated)
        cachedPassword = null;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Scope each role to least privilege and prefer a distinct role per service so a compromise is contained and auditable. Cache fetched secrets briefly in memory to avoid hitting the secrets store on every request, but refresh on an authentication failure so rotation is picked up immediately. Use the AWS SDK default credentials provider chain everywhere and never instantiate static credentials providers. For ECS, understand the distinction between task role and execution role and never over-privilege the execution role.
